{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_0144x4nYr9CkTxMYPuKuAowW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the codebase. Let me now create the planning artifacts for QuantAgent-vna (Triple Screen Strategy).\n\nHere's what I need to produce:\n1. **RQ doc** — `docs/01_requirements/QuantAgent-vna-RQ-triple-screen-strategy.md`\n2. **DS doc** — `docs/03_design/QuantAgent-vna-DS-triple-screen-strategy.md`\n3. **AC doc** — `docs/05_acceptance_tests/QuantAgent-vna-AC-triple-screen-strategy.md`\n\n\n4. **Artifacts** — `result.json`, `run-report.md`, in the artifacts dir\n\nNow I'm diving into the Triple Screen Strategy by Alexander Elder, which uses three sequential filters: a trend filter on the weekly timeframe to identify the dominant direction using indicators like MACD or EMA, an oscillator filter on the daily timeframe to refine entry signals, and a third screen for final confirmation before executing trades.\n\nFor the M1 implementation, I need to adapt this framework to work with the existing `TradingStrategy` abstraction and the available `kline_data` structure.\n\nSince the strategy receives data on a single timeframe, I'll simulate the multi-timeframe approach by aggregating the same data at different resolutions—using weekly aggregation for the trend filter, the raw data for the oscillator, and examining recent bars for the breakout signal. Given the existing RSI strategy operates on a 4h timeframe, I'll apply a weekly EMA slope or MACD histogram for the long-term trend component.\n\nFor the oscillator screen, I'm using either a Stochastic oscillator or Force Index on the received timeframe to confirm momentum—buying when the oscillator dips during an uptrend and selling when it rises during a downtrend. The entry trigger then looks for breakouts above the last candle high in an uptrend or below the last candle low in a downtrend, once the pullback or rally is confirmed.\n\nNow I'm implementing this as a Python class that aggregates candles into weekly bars, calculates the EMA slope for trend filtering, applies the Stochastic oscillator for momentum confirmation, and executes breakout entries based on the prior bar's high/low levels.\n\nDefining the strategy class... I'm laying out the three-screen logic: the oscillator generates buy/sell signals when the stochastic crosses key thresholds in the right trend direction, then the trigger screen confirms entry only when price breaks the prior bar's extremes. I need at least 70 candles of historical data to calculate everything properly, and I'm implementing this as a new strategy class following the same pattern as the existing RSI mean reversion strategy.\n\nNow I'm planning the documentation structure—I'll need an RQ document covering the functional requirements like the trend filter, stochastic detection, and price breakout logic, a DS document with the full algorithm details and parameter rationale, and an AC document outlining the acceptance criteria for implementation and integration.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1578,"cache_read_input_tokens":44772,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1578},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"77fd8154-fdaf-4eee-b2a4-b86e3241c0ee","uuid":"a2ef42e7-c8fb-478f-8ee3-00599bf0f970"}
